 b.summary(p,'A 37-year-old woman without relevant prior medical history presented with six months of right-upper-quadrant pain and intermittent subjective fever. Initial acute-phase-reactant assessment was reported negative and liver/bilirubin tests were normal. Ultrasound found an oval, well-walled segment VIII lesion of approximately 500 mL suggesting an abscess; the abstract also reports initial contrast CT. Ultrasound-guided drainage yielded 300 mL purulence, followed by unspecified culture-directed antibiotics and around 50 mL/day output; symptom improvement and decreased purulence led to catheter removal. One month later she developed recurrent abdominal pain, worsening exertional dyspnea, productive cough and fever, with right-basilar crackles, soft abdomen, mild deep RUQ tenderness and no peritoneal signs. Leukocytosis/neutrophil predominance were reported; the table WBC unit/scale is uncertain. Neutrophils were 92.2%, CRP 15 mg/dL, ALT 48/AST 49 U/L and bilirubin 2.2 mg/dL. Imaging showed a loculated right pleural effusion occupying approximately half the hemithorax, persistent segment VII/VIII abscess and suspected hepatopleural communication. A right pleural tube drained 2,000 mL purulence in 24 hours. Right posterolateral thoracotomy, decortication, fistula closure and anterior/posterior drains addressed fibrinopurulent empyema, pleural thickening ≥1 cm and a tract from the right diaphragmatic dome to the right lower lobe, without biliary obstruction. Drained fluid grew E. coli, with specimen site unspecified, prompting piperacillin-tazobactam. Complementary hepatic drainage yielded 100 mL/day and maintained pleural drainage 400 mL/day, both declining. Joint hepatobiliary/thoracic evaluation led to removal of both drains on postoperative day seven, followed by uncomplicated discharge in good condition. Undated CT showed no residual fistula, effusion or abscess; its relation to discharge and longer-term follow-up duration are not given.',[x['fact_id'] for x in p['facts']])
